const bitsCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'bits', label: 'number of bits', min: 1, max: 16, step: 1, value: 8 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'distinct values representable', expr: '2^bits' },
    { label: 'largest value storable', expr: '2^bits - 1' },
  ],
};

const binCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'weights: 8 4 2 1', note: 'A 4-bit number has place values 2³, 2², 2¹, 2⁰ — read right to left, exactly as decimal weights digits by 1000, 100, 10 and 1.' },
    { math: '13 ≥ 8 → digit 1, remainder 5', note: 'Turn the 8s-bit on; 13 − 8 = 5 remains.' },
    { math: '5 ≥ 4 → digit 1, remainder 1 · 2? → 0', note: 'Turn the 4s-bit on. Five cannot pay for a 2, so that digit stays 0.' },
    { math: '13 = 1101', note: 'Turn the 1s-bit on. Check: 8 + 4 + 0 + 1 = 13. Every number is just this — which is all a computer ever does.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/foundational/binary-bits">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Computer Science, and it assumes no prior computing at all —
    only everyday arithmetic (multiply, divide, powers of two, which we rebuild on the way)
    and a willingness to count slowly in a new alphabet. Everything else is built here, step
    by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why computers are binary — and why reliability, not simplicity, is the reason;</li>
    <li>convert between denary, binary and hexadecimal in both directions, and use two's complement for negative numbers;</li>
    <li>state the master pattern of the whole subject — <strong>n bits give 2ⁿ distinct values</strong> — and predict the capacity of any box of bits;</li>
    <li>describe how text, images and sound become numbers, and compute their file sizes;</li>
    <li>compare lossless and lossy compression, and choose the right one for a given kind of data;</li>
    <li>add binary numbers by hand and explain what shifts do.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit governs everything: bits have no meaning of their own — meaning arrives when a
    reader agrees on what the patterns stand for. That single idea explains text codes, image
    formats, and most encoding bugs you will ever meet.
  </p>

  <h2>Why two states: the engineering trade that starts everything</h2>
  <p>
    A transistor is a switch with two honest states: on and off. Computers are built from
    billions of them, so everything — numbers, photos, songs, this sentence — must be spelled
    in a two-letter alphabet. That is not a limitation philosophy chose; it is what physics
    offers cheaply. A voltage is never exactly 0 or exactly 5 volts — it is somewhere near,
    drifting with temperature and interference — and a system that only needs to decide "high
    or low?" can be wrong by a large margin and still be right. A system that tried to read
    ten distinct voltage levels per cell, decimal-style, would confuse 7 with 8 every time a
    wire got warm. <strong>Binary wins because reliability beats compactness</strong>: two
    states survive noise, ten do not. Everything in this lesson follows from that one
    engineering trade.
  </p>
  <p>
    One switch holds one <strong>bit</strong> — a single 0 or 1. Eight bits make a
    <strong>byte</strong>, which counts from 0 to 255 and is enough to store one typed letter
    ("A" is 65, or <code>01000001</code>). And the master pattern of the whole subject is
    arithmetic, not magic: <strong>n bits give 2ⁿ distinct values</strong>. Sixteen bits reach
    65,536 (the "16-bit" era of games and sound cards); 24 bits of colour give 16,777,216
    shades — the "true colour" of your screen; 32 bits of memory address reach about 4 billion
    locations (a wall 2000s PCs actually hit); 64-bit processors address memory in spaces no
    machine has filled. Turned around, the same pattern tells you how many bits an alphabet
    needs: enough that 2ⁿ covers every symbol — three bits for eight symbols, seven for an
    alphabet of a hundred or so.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="why bytes stop at 255 — set the bit count and read the ceiling" widget="sliderLab" config={bitsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the two readouts differ by exactly one, and the reason is
    that zero is a value too — with 8 bits you can name 256 different numbers, but one of them
    is 0, so the ceiling is 255, not 256. Watch what one extra bit buys: the capacity doubles
    every time, which is why memory sizes come in powers of two. Every "out of memory", every
    "integer overflow", every year-2000-style bug is this slider failing quietly: the value
    you needed to store simply needed one more bit than the box had.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> bits are cheap, reliable switches; n of them name 2ⁿ things, and
    the ceiling is one below the count because zero takes a slot. Now: how a pile of switches
    becomes a <em>number system</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Number systems: binary, denary and hexadecimal</h2>
  <p>
    Binary is a place-value system exactly like decimal — only the base changes. Decimal
    4,093 means 4×1000 + 0×100 + 9×10 + 3×1; binary 1101 means 1×8 + 1×4 + 0×2 + 1×1 = 13.
    Converting denary → binary is the greedy algorithm the widget below runs: repeatedly
    subtract the largest power of two that fits. Converting binary → denary is even easier:
    add the place values under every 1. A merchant's balance makes the uniqueness visible:
    with one weight of each power of two — 1, 2, 4, 8 grams — any whole weight balances in
    exactly one way, because each power either fits (digit 1) or does not (digit 0). That is
    precisely why every binary pattern names exactly one number and every number has exactly
    one binary spelling.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> binary numbers are "different numbers" from denary ones —
  as if 1101 were a strange object of a different kind. It is the same number as 13, spelled
  differently: thirteen is a count, and 1101 is merely how that count is written with place
  values of two instead of ten. Nothing about the quantity changes; only the notation does.
  Keeping this straight matters, because every later topic — hex, two's complement, colour
  depths — is a re-spelling of one underlying store of bits.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="counting like a computer — 13 in binary, honestly" widget="equationStep" config={binCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the algorithm always asks the same question — does the
    next power of two fit? — and each answer is one bit, read left to right. The remainder
    shrinks with every step, so the process must end, and it ends in exactly four decisions
    for a 4-bit number. This greedy subtraction is the whole of denary-to-binary conversion,
    at any width.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two refinements complete the toolkit. <strong>Hexadecimal</strong> (base 16, digits 0–9
    then A = 10 through F = 15) is not a third counting system the hardware uses — the machine
    only ever sees binary — but a human shorthand, because one hex digit encodes exactly four
    bits (one <em>nybble</em>): 1111 = F, 1010 0111 = A7. Memory addresses, colour codes
    (#FF8000 = red 255, green 128, blue 0) and error dumps are written in hex because a 32-bit
    address in binary is 32 characters and in hex is 8 — and the two-step translation (split
    into nybbles, look each up) is error-proof where reading thirty-two raw bits is not.
    <strong>Negative numbers</strong> use <strong>two's complement</strong>: flip every bit
    and add 1, so −13 in 8 bits is 11110011. The beautiful consequence is that the same adder
    circuit handles subtraction for free (a − b = a + (−b)), with no separate subtraction
    hardware. The cost is a fixed range (−128 to +127 for 8 bits) and the famous
    <strong>overflow</strong> error when a result exceeds the bits available — 127 + 1 in 8
    bits wraps around to −128, the arithmetic version of an odometer rolling over.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> in two's complement the leading bit is "just a sign flag",
  a minus sign bolted on in front of an ordinary number. If it were, the 8-bit pattern
  10000000 would be "minus zero" — and indeed the older <em>sign-magnitude</em> scheme, which
  really does spend a bit on a flag, wastes exactly that code, keeping both 00000000 and
  10000000 for zero. Two's complement drops the flag idea: negative numbers are full
  arithmetic citizens, chosen so that a number and its negation sum to zero by ordinary
  addition (13 + 11110011 = 00000000 with the carry falling off the end), and 10000000 is
  genuinely −128, not a second zero. That is why one adder does both addition and
  subtraction.</p>

  <h2>Text: agreeing on lookup tables</h2>
  <p>
    Text is the easiest encoding to understand because it is pure agreement.
    <strong>ASCII</strong> (1963) assigned numbers to characters: 65 = A, 97 = a, 48 = 0,
    32 = space — originally 7 bits, 128 characters, enough for English and arithmetic and
    nothing else (no é, no 中文, no emoji). The successor, <strong>Unicode</strong>, assigns a
    number to <em>every</em> character in every living writing system — over 150,000 of them —
    and defines encodings (UTF-8 the most common) that store those numbers as one to four
    bytes each, chosen so that plain English text still costs one byte per letter and stays
    backward-compatible with ASCII. The deep point is that a character set is a dictionary
    between symbols and integers; once text is integers, it is bits, and every operation —
    sorting, searching, upper-casing — becomes arithmetic on codes. It also means encoding
    mismatches are real bugs: reading a UTF-8 file as an older single-byte encoding is exactly
    how you get "cafÃ©" instead of "café" — the bytes are the same, the dictionary used to
    decode them differs.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> letters are not "stored" in memory. Only integers are
  stored; letters exist at the moment of interpretation — 65 is the code for A when the
  program treats it as text, and a perfectly good number 65 when it doesn't. The same bytes
  can be a character, a pixel value or an instruction depending entirely on what the reader
  expects.</p>

  <h2>Images and sound: quantising reality</h2>
  <p>
    Both image and audio digitisation are the same idea — <em>chop a continuous quantity into
    small cells, agree one value to stand for each cell, store that value's binary code</em> —
    applied to two different dimensions. Engineers call the chopping
    <strong>quantisation</strong>, and it is how any analog-to-digital converter works: a
    voltage between 0 and its maximum falls into one of many equal cells, is approximated by
    (say) the cell's midpoint, and the cell's number is what gets stored. The stored value is
    slightly wrong — quantisation error — but with enough cells the error sinks below
    perception.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>bitmap image</strong> quantises space: a grid of <strong>pixels</strong>, each
    pixel's colour a number whose size is the <strong>colour depth</strong> (24-bit true
    colour = 8 bits each for red, green and blue). Two dials control quality:
    <strong>resolution</strong> (how many pixels) and colour depth (how many colours each can
    be). File size follows immediately: width × height × depth ÷ 8 bytes. A 1920 × 1080 photo
    at 24 bits is 1920 × 1080 × 3 ≈ 6.2 MB before compression. Digital <strong>sound</strong>
    quantises time: a wave's amplitude is measured again and again, the <strong>sampling
    rate</strong> counting measurements per second (CD quality: 44,100 Hz — chosen because
    human hearing ends near 20 kHz and you must sample at better than twice the highest
    frequency you want to keep), and the <strong>sample resolution</strong> giving each
    measurement its bits (16-bit for CD: 65,536 possible loudness levels). File size =
    rate × resolution × channels × seconds; one stereo minute at CD quality is
    44,100 × 16 × 2 × 60 ≈ 10.6 MB. The two dials trade fidelity against size in both media,
    and increasing either multiplies file size linearly — which is why compression, the next
    topic, is not optional but structural.
  </p>

  <h2>Storage units and compression</h2>
  <p>
    First the units, because the gap here confuses everyone: <strong>KiB vs KB</strong>.
    Memory is binary — 1 KiB = 1,024 bytes = 2¹⁰ — while storage marketing uses decimal —
    1 KB = 1,000 bytes. The gap compounds: a "1 TB" drive holds 10¹² bytes, which is about
    931 GiB, and that is why your drive shows roughly 931 GB. Nothing is missing; two
    definitions of "giga" are meeting. (The ISO/IEC 80000-13 standard created the "i" units —
    kibibyte, mebibyte, gibibyte — precisely to stop the argument.)
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a drive sold as "1 TB" that shows 931 GB is defective, or
  the manufacturer is hiding space. The bytes are all there; the operating system is
  measuring in binary gigabytes (GiB) while the label counts decimal trillions. Divide
  1,000,000,000,000 by 1,024 three times and you get 931.32 — the "missing" 7% is arithmetic,
  not theft.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Compression</strong> exploits redundancy: real data repeats itself, and repetition
    can be described more briefly than it occurs. <strong>Lossless</strong> compression
    shrinks a file and can restore it bit-for-bit — essential for text, code and archives.
    <strong>Run-length encoding</strong> stores runs as (count, value) pairs: the row
    WWWWWWWWWWWWBWWWW becomes 12W1B4W; great for cartoons with flat colour, useless for
    photographs where runs are short. Dictionary methods (LZ77 and descendants, inside ZIP and
    PNG) replace repeated phrases with references to earlier occurrences. <strong>Lossy</strong>
    compression throws information away — <em>permanently</em> — but throws away the
    information humans barely perceive. JPEG discards colour detail the eye is less sensitive
    to than brightness; MP3 discards frequencies and "masked" sounds that psychoacoustic
    research says you will not miss. The ratios are dramatic (10:1 is normal) and the loss is,
    by design, invisible — until you re-save a JPEG a hundred times and watch artefacts
    accumulate, because each pass loses a little more. The rule that tells you which to use:
    if the data's <em>meaning</em> must survive exactly (text, medical records, programs),
    lossless only; if the data's <em>appearance</em> is the point (photos, music, video),
    lossy is usually the honest bargain.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> text, images and sound all end as agreed dictionaries from
    meaning to integers, and compression is the art of describing the repetitions those
    dictionaries leave visible. One layer of the subject remains: doing arithmetic on the bits
    directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Binary arithmetic: addition, shifts, and short cuts</h2>
  <p>
    Binary arithmetic works column-by-column exactly like the arithmetic you know, with
    simpler carries. Add 0110 (6) + 0111 (7): rightmost 0+1 = 1; next 1+1 = 0 carry 1; next
    1+1+1 = 1 carry 1; leftmost 0+0+1 = 1 — result 1101 (13). Each column's rule is tiny
    (0+0 = 0, 0+1 = 1, 1+1 = 0 carry 1, 1+1+1 = 1 carry 1), and that simplicity is the point:
    the same adder circuit, replicated and chained, adds numbers of any width. Two more
    operations show why binary suits machinery. <strong>Shifting left</strong> (every bit moves
    one place up, 0 fills the gap) multiplies by two — 0110 (6) shifted left is 1100 (12) —
    exactly as appending a zero multiplies by ten in decimal; <strong>shifting right</strong>
    divides by two, discarding the remainder. Multiplication by a constant becomes
    add-and-shift: ×10 is (×8) + (×2), two shifts and one add. And the place values double as
    quick tests: a binary number is even exactly when it ends in 0, divisible by 4 exactly
    when it ends in 00 — the same trick as "ends in 0" testing divisibility by 10 in decimal,
    and for the same reason.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why can 8 bits store 256 values but only up to 255?</li>
    <li>Convert binary 101101 to denary, then to hexadecimal.</li>
    <li>A friend says a photo "contains the colours" of the scene. What does a bitmap actually contain?</li>
    <li>You are archiving a program's source code and a concert recording. Which compression do you choose for each, and why?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) 256 patterns exist (2⁸), and pattern 00000000 is spent on
    zero, so the largest storable value is 255. The count and the ceiling differ by one
    because zero is a value too. (2) 101101 = 32 + 8 + 4 + 1 = 45. For hex, split into
    nybbles: 0010 = 2, 1101 = D, so 2D. (3) Numbers — one integer per pixel giving its
    quantised colour; the colours of the scene were approximated into cells at capture time,
    and only the cell numbers are stored. (4) Lossless for the code: its meaning must survive
    bit-for-bit, and a single altered character is a bug. Lossy for the recording: its
    appearance (sound) is the point, so discarding imperceptible detail buys a much smaller
    file with no audible cost.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Binary wins because two states survive noise and ten do not — reliability beats compactness.</li>
    <li>n bits give 2ⁿ distinct values; the largest storable number is 2ⁿ − 1, because zero takes a pattern.</li>
    <li>Binary is decimal with base 2: place values 1, 2, 4, 8…, each used at most once, so every number has exactly one spelling.</li>
    <li>Hexadecimal is a human shorthand for bits — one hex digit per four-bit nybble.</li>
    <li>Two's complement makes the same adder do subtraction; the leading 1 means negative, and −128 is real, not "minus zero".</li>
    <li>Text is a lookup table (ASCII, now Unicode/UTF-8); images and sound are quantised measurements; file size follows from resolution × depth.</li>
    <li>KiB counts in powers of two, KB in powers of ten — a 1 TB drive shows about 931 GiB, and nothing is missing.</li>
    <li>Lossless compression removes redundancy exactly; lossy removes detail humans barely perceive — choose by whether meaning or appearance must survive.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    With numbers, text, images and sound all reduced to agreed bit patterns, the next lessons
    climb the stack: logic gates that combine bits into circuits, then memory and the CPU that
    shuffles those patterns billions of times a second, then the programs that give the
    patterns purpose. Every layer above keeps trusting the two guarantees you can now prove —
    n bits name 2ⁿ things, and the meaning of a pattern lives in the reader, not the bits.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Why is there a limit on compression? Because information has a measurable quantity:
    <strong>entropy</strong>, defined by Claude Shannon in 1948 as (roughly) the base-2
    logarithm of the number of possible messages. A source with 8 equally likely symbols
    carries 3 bits per symbol, and no lossless scheme can beat that average — compression can
    only remove the gap between a symbol's raw size and its true probability. That is why a
    text file zips well (letters are wildly uneven in frequency) and a file of random bytes
    zips not at all (every value equally likely — no redundancy <em>is</em> maximum entropy).
    The practical coders work by the same frequency logic: give short bit patterns to frequent
    symbols and long patterns to rare ones, but arrange the patterns so that no code is a
    prefix of another — that prefix-free property makes a bitstream
    <em>instantaneously decodable</em>, each code recognised the moment it completes, without
    waiting to see what follows. Huffman coding builds such a code automatically from the
    frequencies; arithmetic coding squeezes closer to Shannon's limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other deep current is the trade between number systems and hardware: binary-coded
    decimal survives in financial processors precisely because decimal fractions like 0.1 have
    no exact binary representation (0.1 in binary is an infinitely repeating pattern, the
    reason 0.1 + 0.2 ≠ 0.3 in floating point), and banks would rather pay in hardware than let
    a penny drift. The same halving habit that organises the bit pattern reappears far from
    hardware: binary search finds an item in a sorted list by asking one yes/no question per
    step, halving what remains each time. And the frontier is still place-value: quantum
    computers replace the bit with the qubit, which can hold a <em>superposition</em> of 0 and
    1 — but every classical machine you will ever touch remains, at bottom, a very fast
    arrangement of two-letter spellings of the world. Learn to read 1101 and you have learned
    the alphabet.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Shannon, "A Mathematical Theory of Communication" (1948); Petzold, Code: The Hidden Language of Computer Hardware and Software; ISO/IEC 80000-13 units standard; Cambridge International AS &amp; A Level Computer Science 9618 syllabus (data representation). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

